ne could narrow the race considerably on Election Day.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Kim Reynolds (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Iowa's governorship is open with Kim Reynolds retiring, and State Auditor Rob Sand, a well-funded Democrat, faces Republican Zach Lahn, who upset the party's preferred candidate in the primary. Sand has raised roughly $19.9 million to Lahn's $1.2 million, giving him a dominant advertising position.\n\nLate polls uniformly show Sand ahead, with margins ranging from four to eleven points across pollsters including Republican-leaning firms, and an average near seven points. Prediction markets price Sand's chances at 97 percent or higher, and every major race rater calls the seat a Democratic flip, though most label it Lean rather than Likely. The race sits in a state that still favors Republicans at the baseline, so the polling lead carries some risk of overstatement, but the weight of evidence points to a Sand win.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8753, "margin": 5.7}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8859, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8669, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8657, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8472, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8297, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8734, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8592, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.8769, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.8877, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.8875, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.895, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.8938, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.8853, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.8878, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-10-07", "p_dem": 0.8885, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-10-08", "p_dem": 0.9358, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-09", "p_dem": 0.9281, "margin": 8.0